        371.150634765625,
        269.270751953125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "To select an endpoint to feed into the trajectory optimization step, we simply evaluate the position",
      "bbox": [
        100.64303588867188,
        272.4288024902344,
        519.281494140625,
        281.22576904296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "and velocity equations at the time horizon TH.",
      "bbox": [
        88.6870346069336,
        284.333984375,
        293.1026306152344,
        294.0959167480469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "3.2.2. Direct trajectory optimization",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        308.4444885253906,
        272.32763671875,
        317.1617431640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Direct trajectory optimization formulates the nonlinear optimization problem by including both",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        320.3207702636719,
        519.2605590820312,
        329.11773681640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "inputs and states as decision variables (Tedrake, 2009). A collocation or transcription of the dynamics",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        332.2757568359375,
        519.2904663085938,
        341.0727233886719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "is then used to constrain the variables to ensure dynamic feasibility. For this reason, direct trajectory",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        344.2307434082031,
        519.3202514648438,
        353.0277099609375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "optimizers are well suited to accept a set of state variable initializations and are are often more",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        356.18572998046875,
        519.2704467773438,
        364.9826965332031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "robust to local minima than indirect methods (Tedrake, 2009). Here, we use a direct transcription",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        368.1417236328125,
        519.2605590820312,
        376.9386901855469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "formulation of Simpson’s integration rule as described in (Pardo et al., 2016) and formulate a",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        380.0967102050781,
        519.2506713867188,
        388.8936767578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "feasibility problem—our objective is to ensure dynamic feasibility of the trajectory and collision",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        392.05169677734375,
        519.3004760742188,
        400.8486633300781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "avoidance. To solve our direct trajectory feasibility problem, we employ the Sparse Nonlinear",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        404.0066833496094,
        519.2705688476562,
        412.80364990234375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Optimizer (SNOPT) (Gill et al., 2005). Collision avoidance is formulated as a nonpenetration",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        415.961669921875,
        519.230712890625,
        424.7586364746094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "constraint on the occupancy grid. In general, we have found these implicit integration constraints",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        427.91766357421875,
        519.2904663085938,
        436.7146301269531
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to require far fewer knot points, provide good numerical stability, and oﬀer improved performance",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        439.8726501464844,
        519.2705078125,
        448.66961669921875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "over spline-based collocation constraints.",
      "bbox": [
        88.68704223632812,
        451.82763671875,
        266.45965576171875,
        460.6246032714844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Our feasibility problem can be written as",
      "bbox": [
        100.6430435180664,
        463.7826232910156,
        280.80670166015625,
        472.57958984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "min",
      "bbox": [
        94.32404327392578,
        481.765625,
        110.93169403076172,
        490.5625915527344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
